<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Upper Burgundy</h1><p class="st-meta">888 CE – 933 CE · capital Saint-Maurice</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Alpine kingdom of the Welf dynasty, founded at Saint-Maurice in 888, which later absorbed Provence to form the Kingdom of Arles.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the deposition of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a> in 888, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf</a> noble Rudolf, margrave of Transjurane <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a>, had himself crowned king at the abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune">Saint-Maurice d'Agaune</a>. His kingdom covered western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/swiss-confederation">Switzerland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-county-of-burgundy">Franche-Comté</a>, and he held it against the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-of-carinthia">Arnulf</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kings controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-st-bernard-pass">Great St Bernard</a> pass and relied on rich abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-maurice-dagaune">Saint-Maurice</a> and on alliances with local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-geneva">Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sion-switzerland">Sion</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-of-burgundy">Rudolf II</a> was defeated by Swabia at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winterthur">Winterthur</a> in 919, then reached for the Italian crown in the 920s before trading those claims for <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-provence">Provence</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 933 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-of-burgundy">Rudolf II</a> obtained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burgundy">Lower Burgundy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-arles">Hugh of Arles</a>, merging the two realms into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-i-of-burgundy">Conrad the Peaceful</a> ruled the united kingdom under strong Ottonian influence, and in 1032 it passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-upper-burgundy.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Upper Burgundy" width="320" height="340"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (Sidonius) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_Hoch_und_Niederburgund_EN.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>888 CE – 933 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Saint-Maurice</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rudolf I of Burgundy, Rudolf II of Burgundy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Transjurane Burgundy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=kingdom-of-upper-burgundy" aria-label="Kingdom of Upper Burgundy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4811 557 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Upper Burgundy lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5131 813L5139 672M5131 813L5111 886"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M5111 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5131 813h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5131 813h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=910&amp;focus=kingdom-of-upper-burgundy">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
